description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/gap-to-feature.ts, in the region "A slice sequence cut short by a capacity refusal never got its attempt either", deliver a reach verdict after the second direct compose call so its execution row can be graded.

REGION. Ground on the comment beginning "A slice sequence cut short by a capacity refusal never got its attempt either", which occurs exactly once in this file. Below it is a second invocation of resolveFeatureCompose, followed by the line assigning its body to cb. That assignment is the edit site.

THE DEFECT, measured by census. This file invokes resolveFeatureCompose at three call sites and verdict delivery exists at only one. Over the twenty three compose executions since delivery landed, every one logged a successful trace capture so every report carried the execution identifier, yet only thirty percent of rows received a reach tag. The rest passed through the two call sites with no delivery and remain permanently ungraded. Where delivery does run the effect is measured and monotone: the arm's beta rose from nineteen point four eight to twenty eight point four eight while alpha stayed flat, and its implied success rate fell from seventy three point six percent to sixty five point six percent. The undelivered majority is the remaining constraint.

WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE. Both halves are in scope at the insertion point: the report carries the trace store's execution identifier on its execution_id field, and the verdict is the report's ok field. This mirrors the delivery already present at the first call site. It is fire and forget with a bounded timeout and a swallowing catch, so a trace-store problem cannot affect the compose result, and the function's return value is untouched.

Perform exactly one replacement. Find the sole occurrence of this line:
  const cb = compose.body as Record<string, unknown>;

Replace it with these lines:
  const cb = compose.body as Record<string, unknown>;
  try {
    const reachId2 = typeof cb["execution_id"] === "string" ? (cb["execution_id"] as string) : "";
    if (reachId2.length > 0) {
      const reachEndpoint2 = process.env["METABOB_ENDPOINT"] ?? "http://127.0.0.1:8080";
      const reachKey2 = process.env["METABOB_API_KEY"] ?? "";
      void fetch(`${reachEndpoint2}/v2/activities/execution-traces/reach`, {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json", ...(reachKey2 ? { Authorization: `ApiKey ${reachKey2}` } : {}) },
        body: JSON.stringify({ execution_id: reachId2, reached: cb["ok"] === true }),
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000),
      }).catch(() => { /* grading must never affect the compose result */ });
    }
  } catch { /* verdict delivery is best effort */ }

The first line is reproduced verbatim so every downstream use of cb is unchanged. Distinct local names are used so this cannot collide with the delivery block at the earlier call site. No imports are added: fetch is global and the API key is already read from the environment elsewhere in this file.

One replacement, one file, inside the named region. Change nothing else.

